We know that the cosine function is periodic with a period of 360°. Therefore,
\begin{align*}
\cos 150^\circ &= \cos (45^\circ + 105^\circ) \\
&= \cos 45^\circ \cos 105^\circ - \sin 45^\circ \sin 105^\circ.
\end{align*}
Next, we use the sum and difference formulas to find the value. We have
\begin{align*}
\cos 45^\circ &= \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\
\sin 45^\circ &= \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\
\cos 105^\circ &= \cos (60^\circ + 45^\circ) = \cos 60^\circ \cos 45^\circ - \sin 60^\circ \sin 45^\circ = \frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} - \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \cdot \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} = -\frac{\sqrt{6}}{4}, \\
\sin 105^\circ &= \sin (60^\circ + 45^\circ) = \sin 60^\circ \cos 45^\circ + \cos 60^\circ \sin 45^\circ = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \cdot \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} + \frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} = \frac{\sqrt{6} + \sqrt{2}}{4}.
\end{align*}
Substituting these values into the expression for \(\cos 150^\circ\), we get
\begin{align*}
\cos 150^\circ &= \left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right) \left(-\frac{\sqrt{6}}{4}\right) - \left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right) \left(\frac{\sqrt{6} + \sqrt{2}}{4}\right) \\
&= -\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} - \frac{\sqrt{3} + 2}{4} \\
&= \boxed{-\frac{3\sqrt{3} + 2}{4}}.
\end{align*}
The answer is: -\frac{3\sqrt{3}+2}{4}
